turns.text:
If the connection drops mid-write, the transaction is implicitly aborted by the database when the connection closes. Here's the sequence:

1. Query fails with a connection error when trying to execute BEGIN, the write, or COMMIT
2. The catch block tries to ROLLBACK on the dead connection, which also fails and throws
3. That second error masks the original one and propagates to the route handler
4. The route handler has no error handling, so Express sends a 500 with no body

The ROLLBACK is redundant when the connection is already gone—the database cleans up automatically. A better approach is to catch and ignore ROLLBACK errors:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (_) {}
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error and still attempts rollback without letting a dead connection hide what went wrong.

