turns.text:
If the connection drops mid-query, the query fails with an error. That error is caught, then the catch block tries to run `ROLLBACK` on the same dead connection, which throws its own error. This second error propagates up, masking the original one. The finally block releases the dead connection back to the pool.

The real problems: you lose the actual error, and the pool now contains a dead connection that causes issues for the next request using it. Plus the transaction state in the database is uncertain—the server may have started executing your query before the connection died.

The fix is to handle the ROLLBACK separately and always preserve the original error:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // Connection is dead; rollback failed. Log but don't suppress original error.
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error and handles the case where ROLLBACK itself fails on a dead connection.

