turns.text:
Walk through the write() function: `pool.connect()` succeeds, `BEGIN` succeeds, then the UPDATE query fails with a connection error.

The catch block runs and tries `await client.query("ROLLBACK")` on the dead connection. This fails with its own error — ROLLBACK can't be sent over a severed connection.

That ROLLBACK error is thrown instead of the original error, so the caller sees the wrong failure reason. Meanwhile, the finally block releases the (now-dead) connection back to the pool.

On the server side, PostgreSQL implicitly rolls back the incomplete transaction when the connection closes, so data isn't corrupted, but the client loses visibility into what actually happened.

The fix is to handle connection errors in the catch block — either wrap the ROLLBACK in its own try-catch to suppress that error, or check connection state before attempting it:

```javascript
catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // Connection already dead; ignore rollback failure
  }
  throw err;  // Throw the original error
}
```

This preserves the original error for the caller while safely handling the dead-connection case.

